+### DoubleField
+#### definition
+- [S1] A framework combining surface field and radiance field for high-fidelity human reconstruction and rendering, bridging them via a shared feature embedding and surface-guided sampling strategy.
+- [S1] The core representation is a joint implicit function $f_{db}$ that bridges the surface field and the radiance field, allowing shared learning space for geometry and radiance reconstruction.
+#### step
+- [S1] Build associations between surface and radiance fields using a feature embedding shared by these fields in the network architecture.
+- [S1] Apply a surface-guided sampling strategy to disentangle the geometry component from the appearance modeling.
+#### parameter
+- [S1] Input resolution: Ultra-high resolution images, e.g., 4K, are used as input to the method.
+#### comparison
+- [S1] Unlike PIFu which isolates geometry and texture making texture learning space discontinuous, DoubleField enables joint implicit function learning.
+- [S1] Unlike NeRF which entangles density and color leading to inconsistent geometry with sparse views, DoubleField imposes geometry constraints on the radiance field.
+
 ## Background
 The motivation for hybrid representations arises from the limitations of pure volumetric NeRFs, which suffer from slow convergence due to empty space sampling and ambiguous geometry [7358-neural-surfel-radiance-fields.md][9732-unified-neural-implicit-surface-and-radiance-field-rendering.md]. SurfelNeRF [7358-neural-surfel-radiance-fields.md] was introduced to enable online photorealistic reconstruction by leveraging explicit surfels and differentiable rasterization. UNISURF [9732-unified-neural-implicit-surface-and-radiance-field-rendering.md] was developed to unify implicit surface rendering with volume rendering capabilities, allowing for accurate surface reconstruction without per-pixel masks. These methods build on [Neural Radiance Fields](4972-neural-radiance-fields.md) by introducing geometric priors to guide the radiance field optimization.
 
